(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-04-diagnosis-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 4: Diagnosis Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the sensitivity of facial pain for sinusitis in this study?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity = 49/95 = 52% — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.1"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the specificity?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Specificity = 73/152 = 48% — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.2"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nIf the doctor thought the patient had sinusitis because the patient had facial pain, for what percent of patients would she be correct?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Positive predictive value = 49/128 = 38% — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.3"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nIf the doctor thought the patient did not have sinusitis because the patient did not have facial pain, for what percent of patients would she be correct?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Negative predictive value = 73/119 = 61% — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.4"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nHow common was sinusitis in this study?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prevalence of sinusitis in this practice = 95/247 = 38% — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.5"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the posttest probability of sinusitis in patients with facial pain in this study?","choices":["38%","48%","52%","61%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. 1. Calculate the positive likelihood ratio: LR+ = sensitivity / (1 - specificity) = 0.52 / (1 - 0.48) = 1.0. 2. Convert pretest probability to odds: 0.38 / (1 - 0.38) = 0.61. 3. Calculate posttest odds: 0.61 × 1.0 = 0.61. 4. Convert posttest odds to probability: 0.61 / (1 + 0.61) = 0.38, or 38%. Another, simpler approach is that posttest probability equals positive predictive value. Both the positive and negative likelihood ratios (LRs) for facial pain were 1.0. When clinicians asked several other questions about patient symptoms and took physical examination results into account, LRs for their overall clinical impressions as to whether patients had sinusitis were as follows: “high probability,” LR 4.7; “intermediate probability,” LR 1.4; and “low probability,” LR 0.4. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.6"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the probability of sinusitis in patients assigned a “high probability” by clinicians?","choices":["∼10%","∼20%","∼45%","∼75%","∼90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The LR for “high probability” of sinusitis was 4.7 and the pretest probability (prevalence) was 38%. You can use one of the three methods to determine the posttest probability of sinusitis among patients with “high probability” of sinusitis: (1) the mathematical approach outlined in Figure 4.11A (or on websites), (2) using a nomogram (Fig. 4.11B), or (3) using the bedside “rule of thumb” approach outlined in Table 4.4. 1. Mathematical approach: Pretest odds = 0.38/(1 - 0.38) = 0.61 LR+ × pretest odds = 4.7 × 0.61 = 2.9 Posttest probability = 2.9/(1 + 2.9) = 0.74 or 74% 2. Nomogram: Put ruler on 38% for prevalence and 4.7 for LR; it crosses the posttest number at approximately 75%. 3. Bedside “rule of thumb”: An LR of 4.7 (close to 5) increases the probability of sinusitis approximately 30% points, from 38% to 68%. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.7"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the probability of sinusitis in patients assigned an “intermediate probability” by clinicians?","choices":["∼10%","∼20%","∼45%","∼75%","∼90%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ∼45%. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.8"},{"q":"A study was made of symptoms and physical findings in 247 patients evaluated for sinusitis. The final diagnosis was made according to x-ray findings (gold standard). Ninety-five patients had sinusitis, and 49 of them also had facial pain. One hundred fifty-two did not have sinusitis, and 79 of these patients had facial pain.\n\nWhat is the probability of sinusitis in patients assigned a “low probability” by clinicians?","choices":["∼10%","∼20%","∼45%","∼75%","∼90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ∼20%. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.9"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is most correct?","choices":["Using diagnostic tests in parallel increases specificity and lowers positive predictive value.","Using diagnostic tests in series increases sensitivity and lowers positive predictive value.","Using diagnostic tests in parallel increases sensitivity and positive predictive value.","Using diagnostic tests in series increases specificity and positive predictive value."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Requiring two independent tests to be abnormal, that is, using them in series, increases specificity and positive predictive value. However, this approach lowers sensitivity; therefore, B is incorrect. Using tests in parallel increases sensitivity (making A incorrect) but usually decreases positive predictive value (making C incorrect). — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 4.11"}]};
